WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.820
How do fork, pipes and

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:04.120
zombie cleanup connect? Fork creates

3
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.120
a child execution context and

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.760
duplicates descriptor references, while pipes

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.700
supply communication endpoints. Exit and

6
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.880
wait govern process completion bookkeeping

7
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.120
rather than automatically erasing every

8
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:16.860
shared reference.

9
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.280
After a successful fork, the

10
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.200
parent receives the child identifier

11
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.100
and the child receives zero.

12
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.420
Each process continues after the

13
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.640
call with its own user

14
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.080
execution state. The scheduler decides

15
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.100
which runs next; the return

16
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.760
values do not guarantee a

17
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.660
parent-first order.

18
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.940
For two-way request and reply,

19
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.140
use two directional pipes. The

20
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.720
parent writes the request pipe

21
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.260
and reads the reply pipe;

22
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.900
the child reads the request

23
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.940
and writes the reply. Label

24
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.040
both directions before deciding which

25
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.060
descriptor ends each process should

26
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:50.480
close.

27
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.200
Fork duplicates references to the

28
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.920
open endpoints rather than creating

29
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.640
unrelated empty copies of the

30
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.640
pipe contents. Close the parent

31
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.500
unused request read and reply

32
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.420
write ends, and the child

33
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.580
unused request write and reply

34
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.740
read ends. Retaining a spare

35
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.200
writer can prevent a reader

36
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.880
from observing end of file.

37
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.760
A read on an empty

38
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.300
pipe waits if a writer

39
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.540
reference still exists under the

40
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.940
stated blocking semantics. End of

41
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.580
file requires the stream to

42
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.160
be drained and all write

43
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.440
references to close. Closing one

44
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.600
descriptor is insufficient if another

45
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.180
process still holds a reference

46
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.440
to the same write endpoint.

47
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.540
When the child exits, it

48
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.600
stops executing but can retain

49
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.400
zombie bookkeeping until the parent

50
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.100
reaps it with wait. The

51
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.920
process-table slot is not yet

52
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.760
equivalent to UNUSED. Distinguish stopped

53
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.580
execution, retained status, and eventual

54
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.700
resource reclamation.

55
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.920
If the parent calls wait

56
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.960
first, it can block until

57
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.940
a child exits. If the

58
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.240
child exits first, the parent

59
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.740
can reap the recorded status

60
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.720
later. Both orders should be

61
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.000
explainable from the same lifecycle,

62
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.720
so one observed run is

63
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.980
not proof of a fixed

64
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:02.880
scheduling order.

65
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.700
Things to take note of.

66
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.820
Track descriptor references per process,

67
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.200
close every unused pipe end,

68
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.060
and distinguish an empty stream

69
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.820
from end of file. A

70
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.260
zombie retains completion metadata until

71
00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.380
reaping; its existence does not

72
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.880
mean it is still executing.
